---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { github } from '../lib/links';

const platforms = [
  {
    name: 'Web',
    status: 'In development',
    body: 'The primary application. Runs in any modern browser. Source in scalar-app/web.',
    href: `${github.org}/web`,
  },
  {
    name: 'macOS, Windows, Linux',
    status: 'In development',
    body: 'A native shell (Tauri) around the same interface. Builds on all three today; no packaged releases yet. Source in scalar-app/desktop.',
    href: `${github.org}/desktop`,
  },
  {
    name: 'iOS and Android',
    status: 'In development',
    body: 'The same shell and the same interface. The targets are configured and share one Rust core with desktop, but have not been built or run yet.',
    href: `${github.org}/desktop`,
  },
];
---

<Page
  title="Download"
  description="Where to get Scalar for web, desktop and mobile, and what is available today."
  path="/download"
  eyebrow="Download"
  lede="Scalar is early. There are no packaged releases yet. Here is what exists, what is planned, and how to run it yourself now."
>
  <ul class="not-prose mt-10 grid gap-px overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-border bg-border">
    {
      platforms.map((p) => (
        <li class="flex flex-col gap-2 bg-surface p-6 sm:flex-row sm:items-start sm:justify-between">
          <div class="max-w-xl">
            <h2 class="text-sm font-medium text-primary">{p.name}</h2>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-secondary">{p.body}</p>
            {p.href && (
              <a href={p.href} class="mt-2 inline-block text-sm text-primary" rel="noopener">
                Source on GitHub
              </a>
            )}
          </div>
          <span class="shrink-0 text-xs text-muted">{p.status}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2>Run it yourself today</h2>
  <p>
    Scalar is self-hostable by design. A working local environment needs Node 24, pnpm and Docker,
    and takes a few minutes: clone <code>api</code>, <code>ui</code>, <code>sdk</code> and
    <code>web</code> side by side, start PostgreSQL and Redis with the compose file in
    <code>api</code>, run migrations, then start the API and the web app. Each repository README has
    the exact commands. Sign in works with a magic link that is shown on screen in development
    because email delivery is not built yet.
  </p>

  <h2>Releases</h2>
  <p>
    Packaged builds will be published under <a href={github.releases}>GitHub Releases</a> with semantic
    versions and changelogs. Until then, the main branches are the releases.
  </p>
</Page>
